

README file for PALASM 4 version 1.5
----------------------------------------------------------------

1. Simulation modeling problems:
    -- It is recommended that users bring all registered
       or latched outputs to a known state with a vector
       at the beginning of simulation that
       initializes set/reset/clocks/OE.
    -- In-circuit loading of MACH internal registers and observa-
       tion of stored values is not supported at this time.

2. DOS 5.0 & EXTENDED memory PALASM

   To run the extended memory version of PALASM 4 version 1.4
   under DOS 5.0 a memory manager is required:
        - PALASM 4 is not a WINDOWS program ( a separate memory
          manager is required ) and should be run as a
          standalone program.
        - HIMEM.SYS is not sufficient by itself; another memory
          manager is still required. When no other memory manager
          used, BIOS errors are issued by HIMEM's DOS extender
          routines.

   After installation is complete the CONFIG.SYS file should be
   changed to configure the system properly. The following two
   sample configurations provide MS-DOS compatibility when
   used with the Microsoft EMM386  and Quarterdeck Office Systems
   QEMM memory managers.  They have been tested on a system
   configured with most utilities and device drivers in \sys
   directory and the mouse driver in a \syslib directory.  Users
   will probably have to specify different directories for their
   own systems and they should not use the /2 parameter when
   loading their mouse driver unless their mouse is attached to
   communication port 2.

    I. CONFIG.SYS File Using Microsoft EMM386 & HIMEM.SYS:

      DEVICE = C:\SYS\SETVER.EXE
      DEVICE= C:\SYS\HIMEM.SYS
      DOS=high,umb
      device=c:\sys\emm386.exe 2048 m9
      break = on
      buffers = 10
      files =35
      device=c:\sys\smartdrv.sys 2048 1024
      devicehigh=c:\sys\ansi.sys
      devicehigh=syslib\msmouse.sys /2
      shellc:\command.com /e:1512 /p

    II. CONFIG.SYS File Using Quarterdeck QEMM
        ( version 5.13 or higher ) & Microsoft HIMEM.SYS:

      DEVICE = C:\SYS\SETVER.EXE
      DEVICE= C:\QEMM386.SYS RAM MAPS=12 HANDLES=96 NS
      break = on
      buffers = 10
      files =35
      device=c:\loadhi.sys /r:2 c:\sys\smartdrv.sys 2048 1024
      device=c:\loadhi.sys /r:1 c:\sys\ansi.sys
      device=c:\loadhi.sys /r:1 c:\syslib\msmouse.sys /2
      shellc:\command.com /e:1512 /p


3. MINIMIZE may run out of memory in two ways:
      ---  64K segment problem. In the new MINIMIZE program
           furnished with this release, the 64 segment problem
           does not typically happen unless the number of
           variables in an equation is very high -- say 25 to 30.
           If the 64K segment problem is encountered due to a
           high variable count for an equation, the workaround
           is to manually "gate split" the offending equation;
      ---  out of memory message.  The workaround is to use
           extended memory or the MINIMIZE executable from PALASM
           version 1.2. If the extended memory MINIMIZE
           executable also runs out of memory, then the user
           should select the "FAST MINIMIZATION" option in the
           PALASM 4 "LOGIC SYNTHESIS" menu. Note, that an
           "extended" out of memory usually happens only when
           options DT, gate splitting, and "Best For Device"
           ( polarity ) are all set simultaneously. Therefore,
           an alternate  strategy to the Fast Minimization option
           is to reduce the number of MINIMIZATION options
           selected. Typically, "Best For Device" is the most
           memory intensive of the options.

4. Backannotation of truncated names ( > 14 characters ) does
   not work.  Backannotation of unused pins and nodes can lead
   to duplicate pin numbers in the design file -- which causes
   a failure in PARSE.

5. Group names cannot be used within the Simulation section to
   specify values for a collection of signals.

6.  PARSE may run out of memory on rare occasions even in
    extended memory.  Two designs of this type involved very
    complex CASE statements and many STRINGS. The only work-
    around when this occurs in extended memory is to simplify
    the logic or use another way ( probably boolean equations )
    to represent the desired functionality.

7.  GP errors, ZMALLOC and runtime errors produced by PALASM 4
    programs should be forwarded with your name, phone number
    and .PDS file to AMD Corporate Applications ( 800-222-9323 ).
    Many previously reported errors of this type have been fixed
    in this release.

8.  For a 16V8 device, the REG attribute when specified in the
    PIN statement for pin number 16 causes PALXPT to give an
    error ( X3670 ).  Workaround: For pin number 16, remove REG
    from the PIN statement and use := in the equation for the
    pin.

9.  If a file is too large to view under the VIEW option of the
    menu, then it may be viewed using the editor by selecting
    the "other file" option under EDITOR and supplying the
    appropriate file name. Alternatively, the user may use any
    other ascii editor to view the desired file. Since MACH230
    report files are larger than for other MACH parts, the "too
    large to view" problem is more likely to occur now.


